<body>

<header>
  <h1>☀️ Why Is the Sky Blue? · 天空为什么是蓝色的?</h1>
  <p class="subtitle">Three explanations, one for each level &nbsp;|&nbsp; 同一个问题,三种层次的解释。</p>
  <div class="toolbar" id="toolbar">
    <button data-mode="both" class="active">Both · 双语</button>
    <button data-mode="en">English · 英文</button>
    <button data-mode="zh">中文 · Chinese</button>
  </div>
</header>

<main>

  <!-- ============ 1. Toddler ============ -->
  <section class="audience">
    <h2>🧒 Level 1 · 第一层</h2>
    <div class="pair">

      <div class="card lang-en">
        <span class="lang-tag">English</span>
        <h3>For a 3-Year-Old</h3>
        <div class="kids">
          <p>You know how sunlight looks white? It's actually made of lots of colors — like a rainbow hiding inside!</p>
          <p>When sunlight comes down, it bumps into tiny little bits of air up in the sky. When it bumps, the <strong>blue</strong> color goes "scattty-scat!" and flies all over the place, everywhere, up, down, sideways!</p>
          <p>The other colors just keep going straight down.</p>
          <p>So when you look up, your eyes catch all the scattered blue color flying around. That's why the sky looks blue!</p>
          <p class="big">Blue sky = sunlight playing hide-and-seek with tiny air bits. 🌞💙</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="card lang-zh">
        <span class="lang-tag">简体中文</span>
        <h3>写给 3 岁的小朋友</h3>
        <div class="kids">
          <p>你知道吗,太阳光看起来是白色的,其实它藏着好多颜色——就像一道彩虹躲在里面!</p>
          <p>当太阳光从天上照下来的时候,会撞到天空里那些很小很小的空气微粒。一撞呀,<strong>蓝色</strong>就"哗"地一下四处乱飞,到处都是,上上下下、左左右右!</p>
          <p>而其他颜色就直接一路往下走,飞走了。</p>
          <p>所以当你抬起头,你的眼睛就接住了那些到处乱飞的蓝色。这就是天空看起来是蓝色的原因!</p>
          <p class="big">蓝天 = 阳光和空气小微粒在玩捉迷藏。🌞💙</p>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ 2. High school ============ -->
  <section class="audience">
    <h2>🎓 Level 2 · 第二层</h2>
    <div class="pair">

      <div class="card lang-en">
        <span class="lang-tag">English</span>
        <h3>For a High School Student</h3>
        <p>Sunlight looks white, but it's a mix of all the colors of the visible spectrum — red, orange, yellow, green, blue, violet. Each color is an electromagnetic wave with a different <strong>wavelength</strong>: red is long (~700 nm), violet is short (~400 nm).</p>
        <p>When sunlight enters the atmosphere, it collides with molecules of nitrogen and oxygen, which are <em>much smaller</em> than the wavelength of visible light. This causes a phenomenon called <strong>Rayleigh scattering</strong>, where the intensity of scattered light goes like:</p>
        <span class="formula">I ∝ 1 / λ<sup>4</sup></span>
        <p>That λ<sup>4</sup> in the denominator is the key. Short wavelengths (blue, ~450 nm) get scattered <em>far</em> more strongly than long wavelengths (red, ~650 nm). Plugging in the numbers, blue light is scattered roughly <strong>5–6× more</strong> than red light:</p>
        <span class="formula">(650 / 450)<sup>4</sup> ≈ 4.4</span>
        <p>So as sunlight passes through the atmosphere, blue light gets knocked out of the direct beam and flung in every direction. Wherever you look in the sky, you see this scattered blue light reaching your eyes.</p>
        <p><strong>A few "why not…" questions you might have:</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Why not violet?</strong> Violet is scattered even more than blue. But (1) the Sun emits less violet to begin with, (2) our eyes are less sensitive to violet, and (3) the upper atmosphere absorbs some violet. The result: blue wins.</li>
          <li><strong>Why does the sky turn red at sunset?</strong> At sunset, sunlight travels a much <em>longer</em> path through the atmosphere. The blue light gets scattered away almost completely before reaching you, so what's left in the direct beam is red and orange.</li>
          <li><strong>Why aren't clouds blue?</strong> Cloud droplets are much larger than light's wavelength, so they scatter all colors equally (<strong>Mie scattering</strong>), and the cloud looks white.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="card lang-zh">
        <span class="lang-tag">简体中文</span>
        <h3>写给高中生</h3>
        <p>太阳光看起来是白色的,但它其实是可见光谱中所有颜色的混合——红、橙、黄、绿、蓝、紫。每种颜色都是不同<strong>波长</strong>的电磁波:红光波长较长(约 700 纳米),紫光波长较短(约 400 纳米)。</p>
        <p>当太阳光进入大气层时,会与氮气、氧气分子发生碰撞,而这些分子比可见光的波长<em>小得多</em>。这就产生了一种叫<strong>瑞利散射</strong>的现象,散射光的强度满足:</p>
        <span class="formula">I ∝ 1 / λ<sup>4</sup></span>
        <p>分母里的 λ<sup>4</sup> 是关键。波长短的光(蓝光,约 450 纳米)比波长长的光(红光,约 650 纳米)被散射得<em>强得多</em>。代入数字计算,蓝光被散射的强度大约是红光的 <strong>5–6 倍</strong>:</p>
        <span class="formula">(650 / 450)<sup>4</sup> ≈ 4.4</span>
        <p>所以,当阳光穿过大气层时,蓝光被"踢出"直射光束,朝四面八方散开。无论你在天空的哪个方向看,进入眼睛的都是这些被散射的蓝光。</p>
        <p><strong>几个你可能想问的"为什么不……":</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>为什么不是紫色?</strong> 紫光其实比蓝光散射得更强。但是(1)太阳本身发出的紫光就比较少;(2)人眼对紫光不敏感;(3)高层大气会吸收一部分紫光。最终结果:蓝色胜出。</li>
          <li><strong>为什么日落时天空会变红?</strong> 日落时,阳光在大气中要走<em>长得多</em>的路径。蓝光在到达你眼睛之前几乎被散射殆尽,直射光中剩下的就只有红色和橙色了。</li>
          <li><strong>云为什么不是蓝色的?</strong> 云中的小水滴比光的波长大得多,对各种颜色的散射几乎一样强(<strong>米氏散射</strong>),所以云看起来是白色的。</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ 3. PhD ============ -->
  <section class="audience">
    <h2>🔬 Level 3 · 第三层</h2>
    <div class="pair">

      <div class="card lang-en">
        <span class="lang-tag">English</span>
        <h3>For a Physics PhD</h3>
        <p>The qualitative story is Rayleigh scattering, but the full treatment requires a bit more rigor.</p>
        <p><strong>Scattering cross-section.</strong> Treating an air molecule as an induced dipole in an oscillating field, one obtains the Rayleigh cross-section:</p>
        <span class="formula">σ<sub>s</sub> = (8π<sup>3</sup>/3) · [(n<sup>2</sup> − 1)/(N<sup>2</sup>λ<sup>4</sup>)] · [(6 + 3δ<sub>p</sub>)/(6 − 7δ<sub>p</sub>)]<sup>−1</sup></span>
        <p>where <em>n</em> is the refractive index, <em>N</em> the molecular number density, and δ<sub>p</sub> ≈ 0.03 is the King correction factor accounting for molecular depolarization (crucial for anisotropic molecules like N₂ and O₂; omitting it gives a noticeably wrong cross-section). At STP for λ = 550 nm, σ<sub>s</sub> ~ 5 × 10<sup>−31</sup> m², giving a vertical optical depth τ ~ 0.1 — i.e., only ~10% of direct sunlight is scattered out on a zenith path, consistent with the sky being dim relative to the solar disk.</p>
        <p><strong>Polarization.</strong> The dipole radiation pattern gives a phase function <em>P</em>(Θ) ∝ (1 + cos²Θ), so 90° scattered light is fully linearly polarized. This is why a Haidinger's brush / polarizing filter reveals the polarization pattern of the sky — maximum at 90° from the Sun, zero along the Sun and anti-Sun meridians. Multiple scattering and surface albedo depolarize this in reality.</p>
        <p><strong>Radiative transfer.</strong> The observed sky radiance is not a single-scattering quantity. One must solve the transfer equation with the Rayleigh (plus aerosol Mie) phase function, include the multiple-scattering source function, ozone (Chappuis/Huggins) absorption in the visible, and surface boundary reflection. A single-scattering albedo treatment overestimates sky brightness; full successive-orders or Monte Carlo radiative transfer is needed for quantitative match to measurements.</p>
        <p><strong>Why blue and not violet, quantitatively.</strong> The competition is decided by the product of three factors: the solar spectral radiance <em>E</em><sub>☉</sub>(λ) (which declines steeply below ~450 nm at the top of the atmosphere), the λ<sup>−4</sup> scattering factor, the photopic <em>V</em>(λ) cone response (peaking ~555 nm, falling sharply in the violet), and atmospheric O₃ absorption. The integrated perceptual response peaks in the blue (~460–480 nm), not the violet.</p>
        <p><strong>Related phenomena as consistency checks:</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Red sunsets:</strong> large air mass <em>m</em> = sec <em>z</em> → direct-beam attenuation <em>e</em><sup>−τm</sup> preferentially strips blue.</li>
          <li><strong>White clouds:</strong> size parameter <em>x</em> = 2πr/λ ≫ 1 → Mie regime, nearly wavelength-independent scattering, geometric-optics limit.</li>
          <li><strong>Black sky on the Moon:</strong> no atmosphere → no scattering column, hence no diffuse sky radiance.</li>
          <li><strong>Aerosol/bluer-than-blue skies:</strong> fine aerosols add an Angström-exponent α ~ 2 wavelength dependence; volcanic sulfate loading enhances sky blue (and sunsets) accordingly.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="muted">The one-sentence version for a physicist: the sky is blue because the λ<sup>−4</sup> Rayleigh scattering cross-section of N₂/O₂, folded through the solar spectrum, the <em>V</em>(λ) luminosity function, and ozone absorption, yields a diffuse skylight spectrum whose peak perceptual chromaticity falls in the blue rather than the violet.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="card lang-zh">
        <span class="lang-tag">简体中文</span>
        <h3>写给物理学博士</h3>
        <p>定性地说,这就是瑞利散射;但要讲严谨,还需要更完整的处理。</p>
        <p><strong>散射截面。</strong>把空气分子看成振荡场中的诱导偶极子,可以得到瑞利散射截面:</p>
        <span class="formula">σ<sub>s</sub> = (8π<sup>3</sup>/3) · [(n<sup>2</sup> − 1)/(N<sup>2</sup>λ<sup>4</sup>)] · [(6 + 3δ<sub>p</sub>)/(6 − 7δ<sub>p</sub>)]<sup>−1</sup></span>
        <p>其中 <em>n</em> 是折射率,<em>N</em> 是分子数密度,δ<sub>p</sub> ≈ 0.03 是 King 修正因子,用于修正分子去偏振效应(对 N₂、O₂ 这类各向异性分子至关重要;忽略它会得到明显错误的截面)。在标准状况下、λ = 550 纳米处,σ<sub>s</sub> ~ 5 × 10<sup>−31</sup> 平方米,由此得到垂直路径的光学厚度 τ ~ 0.1——也就是说,在太阳位于天顶时,只有约 10% 的直射阳光被散射出去,这与"天空远比太阳盘面暗"的观测一致。</p>
        <p><strong>偏振。</strong>偶极辐射图样给出相位函数 <em>P</em>(Θ) ∝ (1 + cos²Θ),因此 90° 方向散射光是完全线偏振的。这就是为什么用 Haidinger 刷现象 / 偏振片可以观测到天空的偏振分布图——在与太阳成 90° 夹角(即 90° 散射角)处偏振度最大,而在太阳及反太阳方向的子午线上偏振为零。实际上,多次散射和地表反照会使偏振度下降。</p>
        <p><strong>辐射传输。</strong>实际观测到的天空辐亮度并不是单次散射的量。必须求解辐射传输方程,计入瑞利(加上气溶胶米氏)相位函数、多次散射源函数、平流层臭氧在可见光波段的吸收(Chappuis/Huggins 带)以及地表边界反射。单次散射反照率的近似处理会高估天空亮度;要与实测定量吻合,需要完整的逐次散射法(successive orders)或蒙特卡洛辐射传输计算。</p>
        <p><strong>为什么是蓝色而不是紫色(定量分析)。</strong>胜负由三个因素的乘积决定:太阳光谱辐亮度 <em>E</em><sub>☉</sub>(λ)(在大气层外,约 450 纳米以下陡降)、λ<sup>−4</sup> 散射因子、明视觉锥体响应函数 <em>V</em>(λ)(峰值在约 555 纳米,在紫光波段急剧下降),以及臭氧的大气吸收。三者积分后的主观视觉响应峰值落在蓝色波段(约 460–480 纳米),而不是紫色。</p>
        <p><strong>相关现象作为一致性检验:</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>红色日落:</strong>大空气质量数 <em>m</em> = sec <em>z</em> → 直射光束衰减 <em>e</em><sup>−τm</sup>,优先"剥离"蓝光。</li>
          <li><strong>白色云朵:</strong>尺度参数 <em>x</em> = 2πr/λ ≫ 1 → 米氏散射区,散射几乎与波长无关,处于几何光学极限。</li>
          <li><strong>月球上的黑色天空:</strong>没有大气 → 没有散射柱,因此没有漫射天空辐亮度。</li>
          <li><strong>比蓝更蓝的天空(气溶胶效应):</strong>细粒子气溶胶引入 Ångström 指数 α ~ 2 的波长依赖;火山硫酸盐气溶胶负荷会相应地增强天空的蓝色(也让日落更美)。</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="muted">给物理学家的一句话版本:天空之所以是蓝色,是因为 N₂/O₂ 的 λ<sup>−4</sup> 瑞利散射截面,经过太阳光谱、<em>V</em>(λ) 视觉效率函数和臭氧吸收共同"卷积"之后,所得漫射天光的能谱在感知上的色度峰值落在蓝色而非紫色。</p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

</main>

<footer>
  Source: sky.md (English) · sky2.md (简体中文) — all CSS &amp; JavaScript embedded, no external resources.
</footer>

